<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Habsburg Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1020–1030</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Habichtsburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Castle is a medieval castle on the Wülpelsberg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brugg">Brugg</a> in the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, built around 1020–1030 by Count Radbot and, according to tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Werner of Strasbourg. Its name, from Habichtsburg ('hawk's castle'), was taken by Radbot's descendants, the Habsburg dynasty. The family left it in the 13th century, Bern took it in 1415, and part of it survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/habsburg-castle.jpg" alt="Habsburg Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Freaktalius · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Habsburg_(Aargau,_Switzerland).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
